From f0ad349be24f24d1f75cadef0bf378e8f0424ac6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kel Modderman <kelvmod@gmail.com>
Date: Sun, 15 Nov 2026 08:18:44 +1000
Subject: [PATCH] Run Vulkan cores on OpenGL ES

Kodi only gives game add-ons an OpenGL or OpenGL ES context, so a core
that renders with Vulkan fell back to software or didn't run. Answer its
Vulkan request with a GLES context and a Vulkan device of the bridge's
own, made by the core when it negotiates one, with version 1 or 2 of the
negotiation interface. Each frame the core's image is copied into a
dma-buf image, which GLES imports and blits into the framebuffer, with
two frames in flight. GLES waits for the copy on the GPU through a
sync_file where EGL and Vulkan can share one, otherwise the CPU waits.

The request is only taken on when Kodi draws with GLES through EGL and
the GPU can share images with it, otherwise it's refused as before.
The device is on the GPU Kodi draws with, found by its DRM render
node. libvulkan and libEGL are loaded at run time.

The bridge sits behind an interface with a null implementation, which
CMake picks unless it finds the Vulkan, EGL and GLES 3 headers on
Linux. The test workflow and debian/control get those headers, and the
preference test checks that a refused Vulkan request gets no
negotiation or interface.

Assisted-by: Claude
